-- title:
--   Horizontal primes of the crossing model give finite free W-quotients
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a complete discrete valuation ring: a commutative domain that is a discrete valuation ring and is adically complete for its maximal ideal. Let $\pi \in W$ be irreducible, let $E \ge 1$ be a natural number, and consider the crossing model $R = \mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\,W\,(\pi^E)$, that is, the quotient of the two-variable power series ring $W[[X_0,X_1]]$ (formal power series in `Fin 2` variables over $W$) by the principal ideal generated by $X_0 X_1 - \pi^E$. Let $Q \subseteq R$ be a prime ideal which is nonzero and does not contain the element `const (π ^ E) π`, namely the image in $R$ of the constant power series $\pi$. Then the quotient $R/Q$, viewed as a $W$-module via the constants, is both free and finite over $W$; the two assertions are conjoined in the conclusion, so $R/Q$ is a finitely generated free $W$-module. No bound on the rank is asserted.
--
--   This is the statement that the quotient of the crossing model $W[[U,V]]/(UV-\pi^E)$ by a horizontal prime — one not containing the uniformiser — is finite and free over $W$; freeness comes from torsion-freeness over the discrete valuation ring, finiteness from the fact that such a prime lies in neither of the two branch ideals. It supplies the `Module.Free` and `Module.Finite` structure needed to speak of the $W$-rank of $R/Q$ and of norms from $R/Q$ to $W$, and is used in the length computations for the node–annulus comparison and in the construction of prolongations at special places.
